One exterior edge for fourteen hexagons
======================================

Scope: proposed recovery of the existing source lower clause n(4,6,14)>=36. This note proves the analytic reduction and describes a complete finite parameterization; it is not a completed target certificate.

For a fixed edge uv, the Moore trees have26 vertices and fourteen cross edges. There are D=13 missing incidences on each side. If the exterior Y has t vertices, 2e(Y)=4t-26, so t>=7 and n>=33. Counting hexagons gives14n/3 integral; hence3 divides n. Only n=33 needs exclusion for the lower36 clause.

At n=33 the seven exterior vertices induce exactly one edge yz. The other five have four neighbours among old leaves, with side degrees in{1,2,3} summing to four. The endpoints y,z have three old neighbours each; their side degrees are in{0,1,2,3} and sum to three. No two neighbours of an exterior vertex can lie in one branch, by girth six. Both side degree sums are13.

Let the five nonendpoint left degrees be a1,...,a5 and endpoint left degrees u,v. The corresponding right degrees are4-ai and3-u,3-v. They exhaust the624 integer vectors in outside_degrees01.json. The left and right same-side pair totals F_L,F_R obey F_L-F_R=3-u-v. Summing the acquired root-pair equations gives F_L=f(P_R), F_R=f(P_L), where f(P)=(39-sum d^2)/2. The explicit21 possible ordered pairs are therefore a necessary profile condition, independently enumerable from the degree vectors. No sufficiency is claimed.

A complete boundary enumeration can first partition the thirteen left incidences into b positive blocks, with5<=b<=7. Every nonendpoint is positive on the left; only the two endpoints may have zero left degree. Blocks have size at most three and satisfy the same girth and three root-pair equations as in the accepted independent-exterior components. Order repeated copies of a leaf by increasing block index as in the lambda13 component. Name the positive blocks by first appearance and append7-b empty blocks.

Choose the single exterior edge as an unordered pair of distinct block indices. Every empty block must be an endpoint. Reject only if the installed left edges together with this edge violate girth six, or if an endpoint has more than three left neighbours. The remaining right capacity is3-|B| at each endpoint and4-|B| elsewhere. These are nonnegative and sum to13. Attach all thirteen right incidences to the fixed exterior vertices by the same complete increasing-copy recursion, maintaining girth six and the exact right root-pair sums. At completion every vertex and every edge is present. No further exterior edge or vertex is permitted.

Completeness follows by naming the positive left-neighbour blocks in any actual graph, assigning the possible empty blocks the unused names, and taking its actual single exterior edge. Its complete right attachment is one of the attempted choices. At any stage the distance and pair-sum rejections are necessary monotone conditions. The exterior edge is installed before right edges, so later short cycles involving it are detected by the ordinary distance check. The labels of empty endpoints are immaterial because every unordered edge and every right attachment are retained.

An additional necessary test follows immediately from the exterior edge. On either side its endpoint blocks cannot meet the same old branch: that would form a pentagon through the common parent. This can be implemented as a cheaper preliminary test, but the full installed-graph girth check already includes it. No new performance claim is needed.

The bounded normalized Moore inventory has30,276 deficit matrices,492 matrix orbits and25,925,190 normalized matching products. The complete root traversal subsequently closed with578,417 literal representatives. Completion is a separate obligation and receives no credit from that root count.

For a fixed number b of positive left blocks and left pair total F, their exact size counts are n3=F-13+b, n2=39-2F-3b, n1=3b+F-26. Negative counts are impossible. Append the7-b zero sizes and try every possible endpoint pair; zero sizes must be endpoints. The complementary capacities must have the prescribed right pair total. This is a finite necessary filter on final size multisets. A partial partition can be retained only if it embeds into at least one such final multiset: its size-three count cannot exceed the final size-three count, its size-at-least-two count cannot exceed the corresponding final count, and its part count plus the remaining incidences must reach the final part count. These are direct monotone consequences of adding incidences. They do not replace the explicit endpoint-edge and right-incidence search.

Equal profiles from the single edge
----------------------------------

The endpoints y,z have six neighbours in the old leaves altogether. They cannot share a leaf, which would give a triangle. Nor can two of those six neighbours lie in the same old branch: two neighbours of one endpoint give a quadrangle; neighbours belonging to different endpoints give a pentagon through yz and the branch parent. There are exactly six branches, three on each side. Consequently every branch contains exactly one of the six neighbours. In particular u+v=3 on the left, and the right endpoint degrees also sum to3.

For a nonendpoint of left degree a the difference of its two side pair counts is C(a,2)-C(4-a,2)=3a-6. For an endpoint of left degree u it is C(u,2)-C(3-u,2)=2u-3. Since the five nonendpoint degrees sum to13-u-v, the total difference is3-u-v=0. Thus F_L=F_R. Filtering the already completed root list by equality of its two profiles is therefore complete; it does not require another root traversal. Equivalently, the boundary visitor may reject unequal profiles and require that its chosen endpoint block sizes sum to3. The complete endpoint-edge and right-incidence visitor is otherwise unchanged.

The first boundary edition and its bounded execution are preserved. The proposed equal-profile edition is a separate file and uses no output from an incomplete run as a premise. A brief independent reading by l044 checked the six-branch argument; that preliminary reading is not a full certificate review.
